1
00:00:00,400 --> 00:00:03,365
What if you could tell a drone

2
00:00:03,365 --> 00:00:06,329
“find the person in the brown jacket,”

3
00:00:06,329 --> 00:00:08,447
and it simply flew there?

4
00:00:08,447 --> 00:00:11,835
Vision-language navigation is still mostly flown as waypoints.

5
00:00:11,835 --> 00:00:13,529
This is Towards Realistic

6
00:00:13,529 --> 00:00:14,800
UAV Vision-Language Navigation.

7
00:00:16,000 --> 00:00:20,212
Ground navigation assumes a graph of discrete moves.

8
00:00:20,212 --> 00:00:24,425
A drone lives in six degrees of freedom:

9
00:00:24,425 --> 00:00:27,584
continuous motion, inertia, attitude, and obstacles.

10
00:00:27,584 --> 00:00:30,217
Simplified simulators hide that gap.

11
00:00:30,217 --> 00:00:32,850
Real missions need continuous flight.

12
00:00:34,050 --> 00:00:36,470
OpenUAV closes the loop.

13
00:00:36,470 --> 00:00:40,705
Built on Unreal Engine Four and AirSim:

14
00:00:40,705 --> 00:00:42,520
twenty-two high-fidelity scenes,

15
00:00:42,520 --> 00:00:45,545
configurable sensors, reproducible parallel simulation,

16
00:00:45,545 --> 00:00:50,990
and a human control interface that records continuous flights.

17
00:00:52,190 --> 00:00:56,211
Human pilots fly each trajectory;

18
00:00:56,211 --> 00:01:00,232
sensor streams are recorded asynchronously.

19
00:01:00,232 --> 00:01:05,861
GPT-4 writes target descriptions, checked by humans.

20
00:01:05,861 --> 00:01:11,490
12,149 trajectories, 89 object categories, 22 scenes.

21
00:01:12,690 --> 00:01:15,836
UAV-Need-Help turns assistance into a benchmark.

22
00:01:15,836 --> 00:01:17,934
Level one: full guidance.

23
00:01:17,934 --> 00:01:21,604
Level two: help only at critical states.

24
00:01:21,604 --> 00:01:24,226
Level three: collision warnings only.

25
00:01:24,226 --> 00:01:29,470
Search from the description, the scene, and the available help.

26
00:01:30,670 --> 00:01:33,178
The method is hierarchical.

27
00:01:33,178 --> 00:01:38,821
An MLLM fuses the instruction with five RGB views.

28
00:01:38,821 --> 00:01:41,955
First: a far target pose.

29
00:01:41,955 --> 00:01:47,598
Then: a fine six-DoF trajectory in continuous action space.

30
00:01:47,598 --> 00:01:51,360
Backtracking data aggregation adds recovery trajectories.

31
00:01:52,560 --> 00:01:55,611
Test Seen, level-one assistant:

32
00:01:55,611 --> 00:02:01,713
17.45% success, more than twice the CMA baseline.

33
00:02:01,713 --> 00:02:04,764
Under the same protocol,

34
00:02:04,764 --> 00:02:09,340
OSR rises from 18.72% to 48.87%.

35
00:02:10,540 --> 00:02:14,113
The same policy transfers without retraining.

36
00:02:14,113 --> 00:02:17,685
22.42% success on unseen object categories.

37
00:02:17,685 --> 00:02:20,663
4.18% success on unseen maps.

38
00:02:20,663 --> 00:02:23,640
Both far above the baseline.

39
00:02:24,840 --> 00:02:28,364
In closed-loop rollouts, the drone searches,

40
00:02:28,364 --> 00:02:31,889
re-observes when the target is ambiguous,

41
00:02:31,889 --> 00:02:33,651
and avoids obstacles.

42
00:02:33,651 --> 00:02:38,350
Simulation results; real flights depend on site conditions.

43
00:02:39,550 --> 00:02:43,828
OpenUAV, UAV-Need-Help, and a hierarchical navigation LLM.

44
00:02:43,828 --> 00:02:47,495
One platform. One benchmark. One policy.

45
00:02:47,495 --> 00:02:51,162
Code, data, and benchmark are open.

46
00:02:51,162 --> 00:02:55,440
Scan the code to read the preprint.

